  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Showery and stormy pattern continues through\nSaturday night. \n\nA quasi-stationary cold front will stretch across the region, \nanchoring itself from northern Texas through Kentucky into \ncentral West Virginia and northern Virginia. Early this morning,\nan initial wave riding along this boundary will push showers \nthrough northern West Virginia and into Maryland. Once this lead\nwave and its rain clear to the east, the front will sink \nslightly further south, allowing showers to drift over \nGreenbrier and the Allegheny Highlands around sunrise. \nMeanwhile, a second wave lingering over Memphis, Tennessee, will\nbegin tracking just north of the local area. This system will \npush the frontal boundary even further south while interacting \nwith intense afternoon heat in the upper 80s to mid-90s and \noppressive dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s. This \ndestabilization will trigger showers and scattered thunderstorms\nthroughout the afternoon and evening.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has placed areas along and east of \nthe Blue Ridge under a Marginal Risk for severe weather today. \nAlthough the storms will lack organization, they will remain \ncapable of producing locally damaging wind gusts. Looking ahead,\na third and final wave will arrive on Saturday, bringing \nwidespread rainfall. While severe potential will persist into \nSaturday, its exact placement will depend on whether the \nboundary sinks fully south of the area or stalls overhead. Flash\nflooding will pose a localized threat with each passing wave, \nwith the risk peaking during Saturday's rain as the ground \nbecomes thoroughly saturated.\n\nSunday through Tuesday look quieter with mostly dry weather, \nand this quiet stretch may even last longer than that. We will \nsee a similar set up with a slow-moving, strung out front to our\nnorth for mid week into the end of next week.",
